{"title":"Nuclear Fusion","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Nuclear_Fusion\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Nuclear Fusion</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#The_Fusion_Reaction\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The Fusion Reaction</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Why_Does_Fusion_Release_Energy?\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Why Does Fusion Release Energy?</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Fusion_in_Stars\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Fusion in Stars</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Conditions_for_Fusion\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Conditions for Fusion</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#The_Lawson_Criterion\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">The Lawson Criterion</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Confinement_Methods\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Confinement Methods</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Magnetic_Confinement_(Tokamaks)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Magnetic Confinement (Tokamaks)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Inertial_Confinement_(ICF)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Inertial Confinement (ICF)</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Fusion_vs._Fission\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Fusion vs. Fission</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Thermonuclear_Weapons\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Thermonuclear Weapons</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Example_Calculation\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Example Calculation</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Challenges\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Challenges</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Recent_Advances\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Recent Advances</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Nuclear_Fusion\">Nuclear Fusion</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Nuclear Fusion\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b>Nuclear fusion</b> is a nuclear reaction in which two light atomic nuclei combine to form a heavier nucleus, releasing an enormous amount of energy. Fusion powers the Sun and all stars and is the principle behind the most powerful man-made explosions—<i>thermonuclear</i> or <i>hydrogen bombs</i>.\n</p><p>Fusion is also being studied as a clean and nearly limitless energy source.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_Fusion_Reaction\">The Fusion Reaction</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: The Fusion Reaction\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The most studied fusion reaction involves isotopes of hydrogen:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/c/91c85cd2f4fabb04d4e8d27f778436e5a90fe8aa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{}^2_1\\text{H} + {}^3_1\\text{H} \\rightarrow {}^4_2\\text{He} + {}^1_0n + 17.6\\ \\text{MeV}\\]\" width=\"252\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>This means:\n</p>\n<ul><li><b>Deuterium</b> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/9/c29de9b6f5bcd5c904938e3f57842509e4a6b154.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${}^2_1\\text{H}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"22\" height=\"21\" />) + <b>Tritium</b> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c330b5a8660fccf85bfa265bcc775b768f378fca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${}^3_1\\text{H}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"22\" height=\"21\" />) → <b>Helium-4</b> + <b>neutron</b> + <b>energy</b>.</li></ul>\n<p>Each reaction releases about 17.6 MeV of energy, mostly as kinetic energy of the products.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Why_Does_Fusion_Release_Energy.3F\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Why_Does_Fusion_Release_Energy?\">Why Does Fusion Release Energy?</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Why Does Fusion Release Energy?\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Fusion releases energy because the total mass of the products is less than the mass of the reactants. The <i>mass defect</i> is converted into energy via:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/f/daf0e36f477b3d1d7577011f83fffadc74224c64.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E = \\Delta m \\cdot c^2\\]\" width=\"96\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/b/bcb9e62fe9dddb88a478c06a77ee0418140f94e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"14\" /> be the mass difference in atomic mass units (u). Since:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/2/f22e14e44cb2a82e73b5c9e0cddbcfc88fa5c190.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[1\\ \\text{u} = 931.5\\ \\text{MeV}/c^2,\\]\" width=\"157\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>A mass loss of 0.0188 u, for example, gives:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/d/50d3fc48e27a1d1293b965ac01a36e6d6873585e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[E = 0.0188 \\cdot 931.5 \\approx 17.5\\ \\text{MeV}.\\]\" width=\"244\" height=\"13\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Fusion_in_Stars\">Fusion in Stars</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Fusion in Stars\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In stars, fusion occurs under extreme temperature and pressure. The Sun primarily fuses hydrogen through the <i>proton–proton chain reaction</i>:\n</p><p>1. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/d/27d3c2d3894cba72e28764a57d742dc5f24993e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p + p \\rightarrow d + e^+ + \\nu_e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"160\" height=\"20\" />\n2. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/6/9662781d5a8851327f9ebb2cf62f1829f42ee63c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d + p \\rightarrow {}^3\\text{He} + \\gamma$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"131\" height=\"20\" />\n3. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/c/1fc326359b0ea0c3e518fa81dfc7ab4ae079012d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${}^3\\text{He} + {}^3\\text{He} \\rightarrow {}^4\\text{He} + 2p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"175\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Net reaction:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/6/7f61143b12af2c2ad7d3ce17b48b2a1f0701c10f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[4p \\rightarrow {}^4\\text{He} + 2e^+ + 2\\nu_e + \\text{Energy}\\]\" width=\"245\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>Only a small fraction of the mass is converted, but over billions of years, this powers entire stars.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Conditions_for_Fusion\">Conditions for Fusion</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Conditions for Fusion\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Fusion requires:\n</p>\n<ul><li><b>High temperature</b> (≥10 million K) → provides kinetic energy to overcome electrostatic repulsion.</li>\n<li><b>High pressure</b> → increases collision rate.</li>\n<li><b>Confinement time</b> → the plasma must be stable long enough for enough reactions.</li></ul>\n<p>These three requirements form the <i>Lawson criterion</i>, which determines whether a fusion plasma can become self-sustaining.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"The_Lawson_Criterion\">The Lawson Criterion</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: The Lawson Criterion\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The condition for net fusion energy gain is:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/c/76c8597f3a77fbcc020c04020cf8e3e199cf022d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[n \\cdot T \\cdot \\tau_E &gt; \\text{Threshold},\\]\" width=\"170\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>where:\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> = particle density,</li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> = temperature,</li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/a/7caadfa530b197eacbf514c52e659e08584e8895.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tau_E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" /> = energy confinement time.</li></ul>\n<p>For deuterium–tritium fusion:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/b/93b40cac17cddb28cf332ccaa90177be9c7855de.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[nT\\tau_E \\gtrsim 10^{21}\\ \\text{keV·s/m}^3\\]\" width=\"172\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>This tells us that even if temperature is high, if the plasma escapes quickly, fusion won't be efficient.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Confinement_Methods\">Confinement Methods</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Confinement Methods\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span id=\"Magnetic_Confinement_.28Tokamaks.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Magnetic_Confinement_(Tokamaks)\">Magnetic Confinement (Tokamaks)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Magnetic Confinement (Tokamaks)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>In devices like the <b>tokamak</b>, plasma is confined using magnetic fields shaped like a torus. Charged particles spiral along field lines, preventing contact with reactor walls.\n</p><p>Equations used:\n</p>\n<ul><li>Lorentz force: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/d/2dd6cd697807408102ee7f4de3a6bb481c6e52d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\vec{F} = q(\\vec{E} + \\vec{v} \\times \\vec{B})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"146\" height=\"24\" /></li>\n<li>Magnetic pressure balance: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/c/2dca7741f85bdbfbc7fb1b4a065fb203e8f3994a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\nabla p = \\vec{j} \\times \\vec{B}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"96\" height=\"23\" /></li></ul>\n<p>Tokamaks like ITER aim to reach \"ignition\", where the plasma sustains itself.\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Inertial_Confinement_.28ICF.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Inertial_Confinement_(ICF)\">Inertial Confinement (ICF)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Inertial Confinement (ICF)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Small pellets of fuel are compressed by lasers or ion beams. The inertia of the imploding fuel confines the reaction for a brief moment—just long enough for fusion to occur.\n</p><p>The energy gain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> is defined as:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/d/fcd151afb86e4a9c8f3729327459f8dbc19ab0ad.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[Q = \\frac{\\text{Energy output}}{\\text{Energy input}}.\\]\" width=\"151\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>Goal: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/c/93c12e52cc8721c3d4232b439295506305938ad2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" /> for a break-even fusion device.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Fusion_vs._Fission\">Fusion vs. Fission</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Fusion vs. Fission\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"text-align:center\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Fusion</th>\n<th>Fission\n</th></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fuel</td>\n<td>Light elements (e.g. H, He)</td>\n<td>Heavy elements (e.g. U-235, Pu-239)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Waste</td>\n<td>Minimal</td>\n<td>Long-lived radioactive byproducts\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Energy per kg</td>\n<td>Higher than fission</td>\n<td>Lower than fusion\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conditions needed</td>\n<td>Extremely high temperature and pressure</td>\n<td>Neutron flux and critical mass\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural occurrence</td>\n<td>Stars (like the Sun)</td>\n<td>None (rare natural decays only)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example reaction</td>\n<td><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/5/9458e8f5a6895fe8ecf59c32499556a3335d5997.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${}^2_1\\text{H} + {}^3_1\\text{H} \\rightarrow {}^4_2\\text{He} + n + 17.6\\ \\text{MeV}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"248\" height=\"21\" /></td>\n<td><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/0/c70b1139dadb888b02fe7303fdbe85a6ce1a9ba3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${}^{235}_{92}\\text{U} + n \\rightarrow {}^{141}_{56}\\text{Ba} + {}^{92}_{36}\\text{Kr} + 3n + 200\\ \\text{MeV}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"325\" height=\"21\" />\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Applications</td>\n<td>Stars, experimental reactors, hydrogen bombs</td>\n<td>Nuclear power plants, atomic bombs\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Thermonuclear_Weapons\">Thermonuclear Weapons</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Thermonuclear Weapons\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Fusion reactions are used in <b>hydrogen bombs</b>, triggered by a fission explosion that compresses and heats fusion fuel. The sequence:\n1. Fission core explodes,\n2. X-rays compress fusion fuel,\n3. Fusion releases vast energy.\n</p><p>These can reach <b>megaton</b> yields — 1000× more powerful than fission bombs.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Example_Calculation\">Example Calculation</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Example Calculation\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose 1 gram of deuterium–tritium is fused completely.\n</p>\n<ul><li>Number of reactions: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/9/2d925280fb56ec499edcb9c2b6a34bc3ae920bd6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\approx \\frac{1}{5 \\cdot 10^{-27}} = 2 \\cdot 10^{23}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"169\" height=\"38\" /></li>\n<li>Energy per reaction: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/f/bcf17af58f9839c6d3da8b811e7691d3f9704eff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17.6\\ \\text{MeV} = 2.8 \\cdot 10^{-12}\\ \\text{J}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"192\" height=\"17\" /></li>\n<li>Total energy: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/2/c92b581437135a9e5d32e40d866ebec2591994e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E = 2 \\cdot 10^{23} \\cdot 2.8 \\cdot 10^{-12} \\approx 5.6 \\cdot 10^{11}\\ \\text{J}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"294\" height=\"17\" /></li></ul>\n<p>That’s about <b>133 tons of TNT</b> from 1 gram of fuel.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Challenges\">Challenges</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Challenges\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li>Sustaining stable plasma,</li>\n<li>Achieving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/c/93c12e52cc8721c3d4232b439295506305938ad2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" /> energy gain,</li>\n<li>Withstanding extreme temperatures,</li>\n<li>Developing materials that can handle neutron bombardment.</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Recent_Advances\">Recent Advances</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=14\" title=\"Edit section: Recent Advances\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=ITER&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"ITER (page does not exist)\">ITER</a>: International fusion project aiming to reach ignition.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=National_Ignition_Facility_(NIF)&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"National Ignition Facility (NIF) (page does not exist)\">National Ignition Facility (NIF)</a>: Achieved net energy gain using ICF.</li>\n<li>Private startups: Working on compact fusion devices and alternative fuels (like p–B fusion).</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fusion&amp;action=edit&amp;section=15\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Albert_Einstein\" title=\"Albert Einstein\">Albert Einstein</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Nuclear_Fission\" title=\"Nuclear Fission\">Nuclear Fission</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fusion_Power&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Fusion Power (page does not exist)\">Fusion Power</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Thermonuclear_Bomb&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Thermonuclear Bomb (page does not exist)\">Thermonuclear Bomb</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Plasma_Physics&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Plasma Physics (page does not exist)\">Plasma Physics</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Lawson_Criterion&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Lawson Criterion (page does not exist)\">Lawson Criterion</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Einstein%27s_Mass%E2%80%93Energy_Equivalence&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Einstein&#39;s Mass–Energy Equivalence (page does not exist)\">Einstein's Mass–Energy Equivalence</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Tokamak&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Tokamak (page does not exist)\">Tokamak</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Quantum_Tunneling&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Quantum Tunneling (page does not exist)\">Quantum Tunneling</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018101700\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.032 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.049 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 181/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 5482/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:32675:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018101700 and revision id 251921. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}